/* CCNA Gym Cards — part d2 (Domain 2: Network Access) */
export const CCNA_CARDS_D2 = [
 /* ---------- vlan (26) ---------- */
 {obj:"2.1",c:"vlan",q:"Normal-range vs extended-range VLAN IDs",a:"#Normal 1–1005 · Extended 1006–4094\n1002–1005 are reserved legacy VLANs; 0 and 4095 are never usable",s:"Normal 1–1005, extended 1006–4094",
  x:"The 12-bit VLAN ID gives 4096 values, but 0 and 4095 are reserved. IDs 1–1005 are the normal range and are saved in vlan.dat in flash, not in the running-config; 1002–1005 exist for old FDDI and Token Ring and cannot be deleted. IDs 1006–4094 are the extended range. The v1.1 blueprint asks you to configure normal-range VLANs, so expect IDs like 10, 20 and 99 in questions.",
  w:"Normal stops at 1005; vlan.dat holds them, not the startup-config."},
 {obj:"2.1",c:"vlan",q:"Create a VLAN and give it a name",a:"#vlan 20  →  name SALES\nFrom global config; the VLAN also appears if you assign a port to it",s:"vlan 20, then name SALES",
  x:"In global configuration, 'vlan 20' enters VLAN configuration mode and 'name SALES' labels it; the change takes effect when you leave the submode. If you skip this and simply type 'switchport access vlan 20' on a port, IOS creates VLAN 20 automatically and prints a notice. A VLAN must exist on every switch that carries it, including transit switches in the middle of a trunk path, or traffic for it is dropped there.",
  w:"A VLAN missing on a transit switch is a classic silent break."},
 {obj:"2.1.b",c:"vlan",q:"VLAN 1 — what makes it special",a:"#The default VLAN: every port starts there\nCannot be deleted or renamed; default native VLAN on trunks",s:"Default VLAN every port starts in, cannot be deleted",
  x:"Out of the box every switch port is an access port in VLAN 1, the default VLAN. It cannot be deleted, and control protocols such as CDP, VTP, DTP and PAgP are sent in VLAN 1 on trunks. VLAN 1 is also the default native VLAN. Best practice is to move user ports and the native VLAN off VLAN 1 and park unused ports in an unused, shut-down VLAN, which limits VLAN-hopping and accidental access.",
  w:"Default VLAN = VLAN 1; default native VLAN = also VLAN 1."},
 {obj:"2.1.b",c:"vlan",q:"Where unused ports should live",a:"#An unused 'parking' VLAN, shut down\nswitchport access vlan 999 · shutdown",s:"Unused VLAN plus shutdown",
  x:"Because every port defaults to VLAN 1, an unused jack left alone gives anyone who plugs in instant access to the default VLAN. Hardening means setting unused ports to static access mode, assigning them to a dedicated VLAN that has no SVI or routing, and issuing shutdown. Static access mode also stops DTP from negotiating a trunk with a rogue device, which closes the switch-spoofing route to VLAN hopping.",
  w:"Unused port = access mode, black-hole VLAN, shutdown."},
 {obj:"2.1.a",c:"vlan",q:"Make a port a static access port in VLAN 10",a:"#switchport mode access\nswitchport access vlan 10",s:"switchport mode access + switchport access vlan 10",
  x:"An access port belongs to exactly one data VLAN and sends and receives untagged frames. 'switchport mode access' fixes the port as access (no trunk negotiation) and 'switchport access vlan 10' places it in VLAN 10. Without the mode command the port stays in its default dynamic mode and could become a trunk if the far side asks. Verify with 'show vlan brief' or 'show interfaces g0/5 switchport' (Administrative Mode: static access).",
  w:"Mode first, then VLAN — and the mode command kills DTP trunking."},
 {obj:"2.1.a",c:"vlan",q:"switchport voice vlan 20 — what it does",a:"#Adds a tagged voice VLAN to an access port\nPhone tags voice in VLAN 20; PC behind it stays untagged in the access VLAN",s:"Tagged voice VLAN on an access port for an IP phone",
  x:"With 'switchport access vlan 10' plus 'switchport voice vlan 20', one access port serves an IP phone and the PC daisy-chained behind it. The switch tells the phone its voice VLAN through CDP (or LLDP-MED); the phone then tags its own voice frames with 802.1Q VLAN 20 while passing the PC's frames untagged in VLAN 10. The port is still an access port, not a trunk, and 'show interfaces trunk' will not list it.",
  w:"Access VLAN = data, untagged; voice VLAN = phone, tagged."},
 {obj:"2.1.a",c:"vlan",q:"How an IP phone learns its voice VLAN",a:"#CDP (or LLDP-MED) from the switch\nThe switch advertises the voice VLAN ID; the phone starts tagging",s:"The switch advertises it with CDP or LLDP-MED",
  x:"The phone has no idea which VLAN carries voice until the switch tells it. When the port has 'switchport voice vlan 20', the switch advertises VLAN 20 in CDP to a Cisco phone, or in LLDP-MED to a third-party phone. If CDP has been disabled on that port and LLDP is not running, the phone falls back to sending untagged and lands in the data VLAN. That makes CDP the hidden dependency in many voice VLAN troubleshooting questions.",
  w:"Turned off CDP on phone ports? The phones may end up in the data VLAN."},
 {obj:"2.1.a",c:"vlan",q:"#show vlan brief — what it lists",a:"#VLAN ID, name, status and access ports\nTrunk ports are not listed under any VLAN",s:"VLANs with their access ports, but not trunks",
  x:"'show vlan brief' prints one line per VLAN with its name, status (active or act/unsup) and the access ports assigned to it, including ports carrying a voice VLAN. Trunk ports do not appear in the Ports column, so a port that has silently become a trunk seems to 'vanish' from its VLAN. When a port is missing here, check 'show interfaces trunk' or 'show interfaces <port> switchport' next.",
  w:"Port missing from show vlan brief? It is probably a trunk."},
 {obj:"2.2.a",c:"vlan",q:"Make a link a static 802.1Q trunk",a:"#switchport mode trunk\nOn switches that also support ISL: switchport trunk encapsulation dot1q first",s:"switchport mode trunk (after encapsulation dot1q if needed)",
  x:"'switchport mode trunk' forces the port to trunk unconditionally and still sends DTP frames to help the neighbour. Platforms that support both ISL and 802.1Q, such as the Catalyst 3560, default to negotiated encapsulation and reject the mode command with an error until you enter 'switchport trunk encapsulation dot1q'. Access-layer models that only speak 802.1Q (for example the 2960) have no encapsulation command at all.",
  w:"'Trunk encapsulation is Auto' error = set dot1q first."},
 {obj:"2.2.a",c:"vlan",q:"DTP dynamic auto vs dynamic desirable",a:"#auto waits · desirable asks\nauto+auto = access; desirable with auto, desirable or trunk = trunk",s:"auto waits for a request; desirable actively asks",
  x:"Dynamic Trunking Protocol is Cisco proprietary. A port in dynamic desirable actively tries to form a trunk; a port in dynamic auto only agrees if the other side asks. So desirable with desirable, auto or trunk becomes a trunk, while auto facing auto stays access because nobody asks. Many current Catalyst access switches default to dynamic auto, which is why two new switches cabled together often do not trunk on their own.",
  w:"Two passive sides never start a conversation: auto+auto = access."},
 {obj:"2.2.a",c:"vlan",q:"DTP: trunk on one side, access on the other",a:"#Mismatch — no working trunk\nThe trunk side tags frames the access side treats as errors or drops",s:"Mode mismatch with limited or broken connectivity",
  x:"A static trunk facing a static access port is a misconfiguration, not a negotiation. The access side does not run DTP trunking, so the link does not trunk, yet the trunk side keeps tagging frames. Typically only untagged native-VLAN traffic gets through, and only if the native VLAN matches the access VLAN. Other combinations to know: trunk with auto or desirable forms a trunk; access with anything stays access.",
  w:"Static trunk vs static access = mismatch, not a trunk."},
 {obj:"2.2.a",c:"vlan",q:"#switchport nonegotiate",a:"#Stops the port sending DTP frames\nUse with static access or trunk; neighbour must then be set to trunk manually",s:"Disables DTP frames on the port",
  x:"'switchport nonegotiate' turns off DTP on an interface, so no negotiation frames are sent. It is accepted only when the mode is static access or static trunk, never with dynamic modes. On a trunk it means the far end will not be talked into trunking, so the neighbour must also be 'switchport mode trunk'. Disabling DTP on every port is a standard hardening step against switch-spoofing VLAN hopping.",
  w:"nonegotiate + dynamic auto on the far side = no trunk."},
 {obj:"2.2.b",c:"vlan",q:"802.1Q tag — size and fields",a:"#4 bytes: TPID 0x8100 + PCP + DEI + 12-bit VLAN ID\nInserted after the source MAC; FCS recalculated",s:"4-byte tag with TPID 0x8100 and a 12-bit VLAN ID",
  x:"802.1Q inserts a 4-byte tag between the source MAC address and the EtherType/Length field. The first 16 bits are the Tag Protocol Identifier, 0x8100, which marks the frame as tagged. The next 16 bits are the Tag Control Information: 3-bit Priority Code Point (CoS for QoS), 1-bit DEI, and a 12-bit VLAN ID. Because the frame changes, the switch recalculates the FCS. 12 bits is why VLAN IDs top out at 4094.",
  w:"0x8100 = 'tag follows'; 12 bits = 4096 IDs."},
 {obj:"2.2.b",c:"vlan",q:"802.1Q vs ISL",a:"#802.1Q: IEEE, inserts a tag · ISL: Cisco, wraps the frame\nISL is legacy; most current switches support only 802.1Q",s:"IEEE tag insertion vs Cisco full encapsulation",
  x:"802.1Q is the IEEE standard and adds a 4-byte tag inside the original frame, and it supports an untagged native VLAN. ISL was Cisco's proprietary method: it wrapped the entire frame in a 26-byte header plus a 4-byte trailer and had no native VLAN concept. ISL is obsolete and missing from most current Catalyst models, so on the exam the trunking protocol you configure is 802.1Q (dot1q).",
  w:"Native VLAN exists only in 802.1Q."},
 {obj:"2.2.c",c:"vlan",q:"Native VLAN — how it is carried",a:"#Sent untagged on an 802.1Q trunk\nDefault VLAN 1; change with switchport trunk native vlan 99",s:"The one VLAN sent untagged on an 802.1Q trunk",
  x:"On an 802.1Q trunk, frames in the native VLAN cross untagged, and any untagged frame received on the trunk is placed in the native VLAN. The default is VLAN 1. 'switchport trunk native vlan 99' changes it per interface and must match on both ends. Best practice is an otherwise unused native VLAN, which reduces double-tagging VLAN-hopping risk; some designs also use 'vlan dot1q tag native' to tag it anyway.",
  w:"Untagged in = native VLAN; must match both ends."},
 {obj:"2.2.c",c:"vlan",q:"Symptoms of a native VLAN mismatch",a:"#%CDP-4-NATIVE_VLAN_MISMATCH log messages\nUntagged traffic leaks between the two VLANs; trunk still forms",s:"CDP mismatch logs and traffic leaking between VLANs",
  x:"If SW1 uses native VLAN 1 and SW2 uses native VLAN 99, the trunk still comes up, but untagged frames sent in VLAN 1 arrive in VLAN 99 on the far side, merging the two VLANs. CDP notices and logs %CDP-4-NATIVE_VLAN_MISMATCH with both interfaces and VLANs. Spanning tree can also block the affected VLANs on the port as PVID-inconsistent. Tagged VLANs keep working, so only some users complain, which makes it tricky to spot.",
  w:"Trunk up, some VLANs odd, CDP complaining = native mismatch."},
 {obj:"2.2.a",c:"vlan",q:"#switchport trunk allowed vlan add 30",a:"#Appends VLAN 30 to the existing allowed list\nWithout 'add', the command replaces the whole list",s:"Adds VLAN 30 without replacing the current list",
  x:"By default a trunk allows VLANs 1–4094. 'switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20' replaces the list with exactly 10 and 20. To change it later you must use 'add 30' or 'remove 20'; typing 'switchport trunk allowed vlan 30' on its own would leave only VLAN 30 and cut every other VLAN off the trunk. Other keywords are 'all', 'none' and 'except'. This is one of the most common outage-causing typos in real networks and on the exam.",
  w:"Forgot 'add'? You just pruned every other VLAN."},
 {obj:"2.2.a",c:"vlan",q:"#show interfaces trunk — the four sections",a:"#Mode/encap/status/native · allowed · allowed and active · forwarding\nOnly trunking ports appear",s:"Lists trunks with native VLAN and allowed/active VLANs",
  x:"The first block shows each trunking port with Mode (on, desirable, auto), Encapsulation (802.1q), Status (trunking) and Native vlan. Then come three VLAN lists: VLANs allowed on the trunk, VLANs allowed and active in the management domain (they exist on this switch), and VLANs in spanning tree forwarding state and not pruned. A VLAN missing from the last list is being blocked by STP or does not exist locally.",
  w:"Allowed but not active = the VLAN was never created on this switch."},
 {obj:"2.2.a",c:"vlan",q:"#show interfaces g0/1 switchport",a:"#Administrative vs operational mode and VLANs for one port\nShows negotiation, access VLAN, voice VLAN, native VLAN",s:"Per-port admin/oper mode, access, voice and native VLAN",
  x:"This command answers 'what is this port really doing?'. Administrative Mode is what you configured (for example dynamic auto) and Operational Mode is the result (static access or trunk). It also prints Negotiation of Trunking (DTP on or off), Access Mode VLAN, Trunking Native Mode VLAN, Voice VLAN and the allowed list. It is the quickest way to prove whether DTP turned a port into a trunk.",
  w:"Administrative = configured; Operational = what actually happened."},
 {obj:"2.1.c",c:"vlan",q:"Inter-VLAN routing — the three options",a:"#Separate router interfaces · router-on-a-stick · Layer 3 switch SVIs\nHosts in different VLANs always need a Layer 3 hop",s:"Router per VLAN, router-on-a-stick, or L3 switch SVIs",
  x:"Each VLAN is its own subnet and broadcast domain, so traffic between VLANs must be routed. Options: one physical router interface per VLAN (simple but does not scale), router-on-a-stick with 802.1Q subinterfaces over one trunk, or a multilayer switch with an SVI per VLAN and 'ip routing'. The L3 switch routes in hardware and is the usual campus choice; router-on-a-stick suits small sites but its single link can bottleneck.",
  w:"Each VLAN needs a gateway; where that gateway lives is the design choice."},
 {obj:"2.1.c",c:"vlan",q:"Router-on-a-stick subinterface config",a:"#interface g0/0.10 · encapsulation dot1Q 10 · ip address …\nOne subinterface per VLAN; switch side is a trunk",s:"Subinterface with encapsulation dot1Q and an IP",
  x:"Create a subinterface per VLAN, such as g0/0.10, then 'encapsulation dot1Q 10' before assigning the IP address, which becomes the hosts' default gateway. The subinterface number is only a label, but matching it to the VLAN ID avoids confusion. The physical interface needs 'no shutdown'. The switch port facing the router must be an 802.1Q trunk that allows those VLANs, or no tagged frames will arrive.",
  w:"Encapsulation before IP address, or IOS refuses the address."},
 {obj:"2.1.c",c:"vlan",q:"Router-on-a-stick: native VLAN on the router",a:"#encapsulation dot1Q 99 native\nOr put the native VLAN's IP on the physical interface",s:"encapsulation dot1Q <id> native on the subinterface",
  x:"Frames in the switch's native VLAN arrive at the router untagged. To route that VLAN, either add the 'native' keyword ('encapsulation dot1Q 99 native') on its subinterface or configure the IP address directly on the physical interface. If the router's idea of the native VLAN disagrees with the switch, that VLAN's hosts lose their gateway while all tagged VLANs still work.",
  w:"Untagged frames hit the native subinterface or the physical interface."},
 {obj:"2.1.c",c:"vlan",q:"SVI for inter-VLAN routing on a Layer 3 switch",a:"#interface vlan 10 · ip address … · no shutdown\nPlus global ip routing to route between SVIs",s:"interface vlan 10 with an IP, plus ip routing",
  x:"A switched virtual interface is a logical Layer 3 interface for a VLAN. Configure 'interface vlan 10', an IP address (the hosts' gateway) and 'no shutdown'. On a multilayer switch, routing between SVIs also needs the global 'ip routing' command; without it the switch behaves like a host with several addresses and routes nothing. Layer 2-only switches use a single SVI just for management plus 'ip default-gateway'.",
  w:"SVIs without ip routing = addresses, no routing."},
 {obj:"2.1.c",c:"vlan",q:"Why an SVI shows down/down",a:"#VLAN missing, or no up port in that VLAN\nThe SVI needs the VLAN to exist and one forwarding port in it",s:"VLAN missing or no active port in it",
  x:"An SVI comes up/up only if the VLAN exists in the VLAN database, at least one port in that VLAN (access port or trunk allowing it) is up and STP-forwarding, and the SVI is not administratively shut. Creating 'interface vlan 30' before 'vlan 30' exists, or with every VLAN 30 port unplugged, leaves it down/down. Administratively down means someone forgot 'no shutdown'.",
  w:"down/down SVI: check the VLAN exists and has a live port."},
 {obj:"2.1.c",c:"vlan",q:"#no switchport — routed port on a multilayer switch",a:"#Turns a switch port into a Layer 3 interface\nTakes an IP directly; belongs to no VLAN",s:"Converts the port into a routed Layer 3 interface",
  x:"'no switchport' removes all Layer 2 behaviour from a physical port on a multilayer switch: no VLAN membership, no STP, no DTP. You then configure an IP address directly, much like a router interface. Routed ports are typical for point-to-point uplinks between distribution and core switches. Compare with an SVI, which is a virtual interface representing a whole VLAN and stays up while any port in that VLAN is up.",
  w:"Routed port = one link; SVI = one VLAN."},
 {obj:"2.2.a",c:"vlan",q:"VLAN works locally but not across the trunk",a:"#Check allowed list, VLAN existence on both sides, native match\nshow interfaces trunk on each switch",s:"Allowed list, missing VLAN or native mismatch",
  x:"When hosts in VLAN 30 can talk on one switch but not to the same VLAN on the next, walk the trunk: is the link actually trunking (DTP modes), is VLAN 30 in the allowed list on both ends, does VLAN 30 exist on both switches (it must be 'active'), and is STP forwarding it? 'show interfaces trunk' answers all of these in one view; 'show vlan brief' confirms the VLAN exists.",
  w:"Allowed, active, forwarding — read all three lists."},

 /* ---------- l2link (18) ---------- */
 {obj:"2.3",c:"l2link",q:"CDP vs LLDP — the standards",a:"#CDP: Cisco proprietary · LLDP: IEEE 802.1AB\nBoth are Layer 2 neighbour discovery, one hop only",s:"Cisco proprietary vs IEEE 802.1AB",
  x:"Cisco Discovery Protocol and Link Layer Discovery Protocol both advertise device identity, capabilities, port and management address to directly connected neighbours at Layer 2, so they work even with no IP configured. CDP is Cisco proprietary; LLDP is the IEEE 802.1AB standard for multivendor networks. Neither is forwarded past the directly connected device, so you only ever see one-hop neighbours.",
  w:"Mixed-vendor network? Use LLDP."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"l2link",q:"CDP timers",a:"#Advertise every 60 s · holdtime 180 s\ncdp timer · cdp holdtime",s:"60-second updates, 180-second holdtime",
  x:"By default CDP sends an advertisement every 60 seconds and neighbours keep the entry for a holdtime of 180 seconds, three missed updates, before removing it. The Holdtme column in 'show cdp neighbors' counts down from 180 and resets with every advertisement. Change them with 'cdp timer' and 'cdp holdtime' in global config. CDP version 2 is the default on IOS 15.",
  w:"CDP 60/180; LLDP 30/120."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"l2link",q:"LLDP timers",a:"#Advertise every 30 s · holdtime 120 s\nlldp timer · lldp holdtime · lldp reinit 2",s:"30-second updates, 120-second holdtime",
  x:"LLDP on Cisco IOS sends updates every 30 seconds and tells neighbours to hold the information for 120 seconds. A separate reinit delay (2 seconds) waits before reinitialising LLDP on an interface. Tune them with 'lldp timer', 'lldp holdtime' and 'lldp reinit'. The numbers are a favourite comparison with CDP's 60 and 180, so keep the two pairs straight.",
  w:"LLDP is twice as chatty as CDP."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"l2link",q:"Default state of CDP and LLDP on Cisco IOS",a:"#CDP on by default · LLDP off by default\nlldp run enables LLDP globally",s:"CDP enabled, LLDP disabled",
  x:"On Cisco routers and switches CDP is enabled globally and on all supported interfaces out of the box. LLDP is disabled until you enter 'lldp run' in global config; once enabled, each interface both transmits and receives by default. Disable CDP globally with 'no cdp run' or per port with 'no cdp enable'. Turning CDP off on internet-facing or untrusted ports is a common hardening step because it reveals model, IOS version and addresses.",
  w:"CDP talks by default; LLDP needs 'lldp run'."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"l2link",q:"Per-interface control of LLDP",a:"#lldp transmit · lldp receive (and no forms)\nTwo separate commands, unlike CDP's single cdp enable",s:"Separate lldp transmit and lldp receive commands",
  x:"LLDP lets you control each direction independently on an interface: 'no lldp transmit' stops sending advertisements while still learning neighbours, and 'no lldp receive' does the opposite. CDP has only the single 'cdp enable' / 'no cdp enable' per interface. A question asking how to stop a switch from advertising itself on a port while still learning what is attached points to 'no lldp transmit'.",
  w:"LLDP splits the interface switch into transmit and receive."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"l2link",q:"#show cdp neighbors — what each column means",a:"#Device ID · Local Intrfce · Holdtme · Capability · Platform · Port ID\nLocal = my port; Port ID = the neighbour's port",s:"Neighbour name, my port, holdtime, caps, model, their port",
  x:"'show cdp neighbors' lists each neighbour's hostname (Device ID), your own interface it was heard on (Local Intrfce), seconds left before the entry expires, capability codes (R router, S switch, I IGMP, P phone and so on), hardware platform and the neighbour's interface (Port ID). Exam traps swap Local Intrfce and Port ID. It does not show IP addresses; you need the detail form or 'show cdp entry' for that.",
  w:"Local Intrfce is yours; Port ID is theirs."},
 {obj:"2.3",c:"l2link",q:"#show cdp neighbors detail — extra information",a:"#Neighbour IP address, IOS version, native VLAN, duplex\nSame as show cdp entry *",s:"Adds IP address, software version, native VLAN",
  x:"The detail form adds the neighbour's management IP address(es), the full software version string, the platform, the native VLAN and duplex setting of the link, and VTP domain where relevant. It is the standard way to discover the IP of an unknown neighbour so you can SSH to it. 'show cdp entry SW2' shows the same for one device and 'show cdp entry *' for all. LLDP's equivalent is 'show lldp neighbors detail'.",
  w:"Need the neighbour's IP? Add 'detail'."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"l2link",q:"EtherChannel negotiation protocols and modes",a:"#LACP: active / passive · PAgP: desirable / auto · on: none\nLACP is IEEE 802.3ad; PAgP is Cisco proprietary",s:"LACP active/passive, PAgP desirable/auto, or on",
  x:"EtherChannel bundles up to eight active physical links into one logical port-channel that STP treats as one link. LACP (IEEE 802.3ad, now 802.1AX) uses active and passive modes; PAgP (Cisco) uses desirable and auto; mode 'on' forces a static bundle with no protocol at all. The v1.1 blueprint names LACP, so expect 'channel-group 1 mode active' as the standard answer.",
  w:"LACP = active/passive; PAgP = desirable/auto."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"l2link",q:"LACP mode combinations that form a channel",a:"#active+active ✓ · active+passive ✓ · passive+passive ✗\nAt least one side must actively send LACPDUs",s:"active+active or active+passive; never passive+passive",
  x:"A passive LACP port only responds to LACPDUs; an active port initiates. So the bundle forms if at least one end is active. Two passive ends wait forever and the ports run as individual links (shown with flag I in the summary). The PAgP pattern is identical: desirable with desirable or auto works, auto with auto does not. Mixing protocols, such as LACP active with PAgP desirable, never forms a channel.",
  w:"Same rule as DTP: two listeners never start a conversation."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"l2link",q:"Mode on — what can it pair with?",a:"#Only on at the other end\non + active / passive / desirable / auto = no channel",s:"Only another side set to on",
  x:"'channel-group 1 mode on' builds the bundle without sending LACP or PAgP frames. The far end must also be 'on'; a protocol mode on the other side waits for negotiation frames that never come. Static on is risky: if one side is misconfigured the ports may still forward independently while the other side treats them as one link, which can cause loops or MAC flapping. Negotiated LACP catches such mistakes.",
  w:"'on' talks to nobody, so only 'on' understands it."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"l2link",q:"Layer 2 LACP EtherChannel configuration",a:"#interface range g1/0/1 - 2 · channel-group 1 mode active\nThen configure trunking on interface port-channel 1",s:"channel-group 1 mode active on each member",
  x:"Select the member ports with 'interface range', then 'channel-group 1 mode active'. IOS automatically creates 'interface port-channel 1'. Settings applied to the port-channel, such as 'switchport mode trunk' and the allowed VLAN list, are pushed to the members. The channel-group number is only locally significant, so SW1 can use 1 while SW2 uses 5. Verify with 'show etherchannel summary'.",
  w:"Channel number is local; mode compatibility is what must match."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"l2link",q:"Settings that must match on all bundle members",a:"#Speed, duplex, switchport mode, access/native VLAN, allowed VLANs\nA mismatched port is suspended (s) or stays out",s:"Speed, duplex, mode, access/native and allowed VLANs",
  x:"Every member port must agree on speed and duplex, access or trunk mode, the access VLAN (for access bundles), and the native VLAN and allowed list (for trunks). Layer 3 bundles also need every member in 'no switchport'. A port that differs is suspended or left stand-alone instead of joining. The easiest fix is to configure the port-channel interface and let IOS copy the settings, rather than editing individual members.",
  w:"Bundle members are clones; one odd port gets suspended."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"l2link",q:"#show etherchannel summary — flags SU, RU, SD",a:"#S = Layer 2 · R = Layer 3 · U = in use · D = down\nPo1(SU) is a healthy Layer 2 bundle",s:"S Layer 2, R Layer 3, U in use, D down",
  x:"The Port-channel column shows a two-letter code. The first letter tells the layer: S for a Layer 2 switched bundle, R for a routed Layer 3 bundle. The second is the state: U in use, D down. Po1(SU) is a working L2 channel, Po2(RU) a working L3 channel, and Po1(SD) means the logical interface is down, often because no member bundled. The Protocol column then shows LACP, PAgP or a dash for mode on.",
  w:"SU = switched and up; RU = routed and up."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"l2link",q:"#show etherchannel summary — member flags P, I, s, D",a:"#P bundled · I stand-alone · s suspended · D down\nG0/1(P) is a working member",s:"P bundled, I stand-alone, s suspended, D down",
  x:"Each member port is listed with a flag in parentheses. (P) means bundled in the port-channel and forwarding as part of it. (I) means stand-alone: no negotiation partner, so it works as an ordinary port, for example when both sides are passive. (s) means suspended because its configuration does not match the bundle. (D) is down. (H) marks an LACP hot-standby port beyond the eight active links.",
  w:"Want all P. I = no partner, s = config mismatch."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"l2link",q:"Layer 3 EtherChannel configuration",a:"#no switchport on members and port-channel\nIP address goes on interface port-channel",s:"no switchport on members, IP on the port-channel",
  x:"A routed EtherChannel joins Layer 3 ports. Issue 'no switchport' on the physical members before 'channel-group 1 mode active', and make sure interface port-channel 1 is also 'no switchport'; then put the IP address on the port-channel, never on members. If the members stay switched while the port-channel is routed, the ports do not bundle. 'show etherchannel summary' shows Po1(RU) when it is healthy.",
  w:"Layer 3 bundle: IP on Po1, not on the members."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"l2link",q:"How EtherChannel spreads traffic",a:"#Per-flow hash, not per-packet round-robin\nport-channel load-balance src-dst-ip (for example)",s:"Hash on addresses per flow, not round-robin",
  x:"The switch hashes fields such as source and destination MAC or IP to choose a member link, so all frames in one conversation take the same link and stay in order. One big flow therefore cannot exceed a single member's speed. 'port-channel load-balance src-dst-ip' changes the global method, and 'show etherchannel load-balance' shows it. Choosing fields with variety (IP pairs rather than one router MAC) gives a better spread.",
  w:"Two 1G links do not give one flow 2 Gbps."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"l2link",q:"Why EtherChannel helps spanning tree",a:"#STP sees the bundle as one logical link\nNo member is blocked; failover inside the bundle is fast",s:"STP treats the bundle as one link, nothing blocked",
  x:"Without EtherChannel, two parallel links between switches form a loop and STP blocks one of them, wasting half the bandwidth. A port-channel is one STP port, so every member forwards and the bandwidth adds up across flows. If a member fails, traffic is rehashed onto the survivors without an STP topology change, so recovery is much faster than waiting for spanning tree to unblock a port.",
  w:"Bundle it and STP stops blocking your parallel links."},
 {obj:"2.4",c:"l2link",q:"LACP limit on bundle members",a:"#8 active links per port-channel\nLACP can hold up to 8 more as hot-standby (flag H)",s:"Eight active, extra ports as hot-standby",
  x:"An EtherChannel forwards on at most eight active member links. LACP allows up to sixteen ports to be configured in a channel group, with the extra eight held in hot-standby (H) and promoted if an active member fails; LACP system and port priority decide which are active. PAgP and static on do not provide standby members. Member count also affects load sharing: powers of two (2, 4, 8) spread hash buckets evenly.",
  w:"8 active; LACP adds standbys, PAgP does not."},
 /* ---------- stp (22) ---------- */
 {obj:"2.5.a",c:"stp",q:"How the root bridge is elected",a:"#Lowest bridge ID wins\nBridge ID = priority (incl. VLAN as sys-id-ext) + MAC; lowest MAC breaks ties",s:"Lowest bridge ID: priority first, then lowest MAC",
  x:"Every switch starts by claiming to be root and advertises its bridge ID in BPDUs; any switch hearing a lower (superior) ID stops claiming. The bridge ID is the 16-bit priority field followed by the switch MAC address. Compare priority first and only if it ties compare MAC, lowest wins. With all defaults the switch with the lowest MAC wins, which is often the oldest and slowest switch, so root is set deliberately.",
  w:"Lowest priority, then lowest MAC — never highest."},
 {obj:"2.5.a",c:"stp",q:"Bridge priority values and the sys-id-ext",a:"#Configurable in multiples of 4096 (0–61440)\nAdvertised priority = configured priority + VLAN ID",s:"Multiples of 4096, plus the VLAN ID added",
  x:"The 16-bit priority field is split: the top 4 bits are the configurable priority and the low 12 bits hold the extended system ID, which is the VLAN number. That is why priority must be a multiple of 4096 and why the default 32768 shows as 32769 in VLAN 1 and 32778 in VLAN 10. 'spanning-tree vlan 10 priority 4096' is valid; 'priority 5000' is rejected by IOS with a list of allowed values.",
  w:"Default 32768 + VLAN 10 = 32778 in the output."},
 {obj:"2.5.a",c:"stp",q:"#spanning-tree vlan 10 root primary",a:"#Sets priority to 24576, or 4096 below the current root if needed\nA macro run once; it is not re-evaluated later",s:"Sets 24576 or 4096 below the current root",
  x:"The root primary macro looks at the current root bridge priority. If 24576 would make this switch root, it sets 24576; otherwise it sets a value 4096 lower than the current root's priority. It is a one-time calculation saved as a plain 'spanning-tree vlan 10 priority' line, so if another switch is later configured with a lower priority, this switch loses root. Hard-coding 'priority 0' or 4096 is more predictable.",
  w:"Root primary is a one-shot macro, not a guarantee."},
 {obj:"2.5.a",c:"stp",q:"#spanning-tree vlan 10 root secondary",a:"#Sets priority 28672\nBelow the default 32768, above a typical primary at 24576",s:"Sets priority 28672 for the backup root",
  x:"Root secondary sets the switch priority to 28672 so it becomes root only if the primary fails, assuming every other switch keeps the default 32768. Configure primary and secondary on the two distribution switches, typically alternating per VLAN to share load in Rapid PVST+. If a third switch has been tuned below 28672, secondary will not behave as expected because the macro does not adjust for it.",
  w:"Primary 24576, secondary 28672, everyone else 32768."},
 {obj:"2.5.a",c:"stp",q:"STP default port costs (short method)",a:"#10 Mb/s = 100 · 100 Mb/s = 19 · 1 Gb/s = 4 · 10 Gb/s = 2\nLower total cost to the root wins",s:"10M 100, 100M 19, 1G 4, 10G 2",
  x:"Each port has a cost based on its speed; a switch's root path cost is the sum of the costs of the ports it receives from on the way to the root. The classic 802.1D short values are 100, 19, 4 and 2 for 10M, 100M, 1G and 10G. A port-channel gets a lower cost than one member. You can override per port with 'spanning-tree vlan 10 cost 10' or 'spanning-tree cost' on the interface.",
  w:"FastEthernet 19, GigabitEthernet 4."},
 {obj:"2.5.a",c:"stp",q:"Root port selection tiebreakers",a:"#1 lowest root path cost · 2 lowest neighbour bridge ID\n3 lowest neighbour port priority · 4 lowest neighbour port number",s:"Cost, neighbour BID, neighbour port priority, port number",
  x:"Every non-root switch picks exactly one root port, the port with the best path to the root. Ties are broken in order: lowest total cost to root, then lowest bridge ID of the upstream neighbour, then lowest port priority advertised by the neighbour (default 128), then lowest neighbour port number. With two parallel links to the same upstream switch, the last two tiebreakers decide, using the neighbour's values, not local ones.",
  w:"Tiebreakers 3 and 4 use the sender's port, not yours."},
 {obj:"2.5.b",c:"stp",q:"Designated port — which one per segment",a:"#The port on each link with the best path to root\nAll ports on the root bridge are designated",s:"The port on each segment closest to the root",
  x:"Each link gets exactly one designated port, the one that forwards toward the segment and sends BPDUs onto it. It is the port offering the lowest root path cost; a tie goes to the switch with the lower bridge ID. Because the root bridge has cost 0, every root-bridge port is designated. A link between a root port and a designated port carries traffic; any other port left over becomes alternate or backup and discards.",
  w:"Root bridge: all ports designated, never a root port."},
 {obj:"2.5.b",c:"stp",q:"RSTP alternate port",a:"#Discarding backup path to the root via another switch\nTakes over immediately if the root port fails",s:"Standby path to root through a different switch",
  x:"An alternate port receives superior BPDUs from a different switch but is not the root port, so it discards. It is a pre-computed replacement: if the root port fails, the alternate moves to root port and forwards almost immediately, without the 30 seconds of listening and learning of classic STP. It shows as Altn BLK in 'show spanning-tree'. In legacy 802.1D the same port was just a blocking non-designated port.",
  w:"Alternate = backup root port; Altn BLK in the output."},
 {obj:"2.5.b",c:"stp",q:"RSTP backup port",a:"#Discarding port backing up a designated port on a shared segment\nOnly appears when one switch has two ports on the same hub",s:"Backup for a designated port on the same segment",
  x:"A backup port receives superior BPDUs from its own switch, meaning two ports of the same switch connect to one shared segment, usually through a hub. One becomes designated and the other becomes backup and discards. It is rare in modern switched networks, which is why exam questions use it as a distractor. Remember the difference: alternate backs up the root port via another switch; backup backs up a designated port via the same switch.",
  w:"Backup needs a hub — rare outside exam questions."},
 {obj:"2.5.b",c:"stp",q:"RSTP port states",a:"#Discarding · Learning · Forwarding\nDiscarding replaces 802.1D disabled, blocking and listening",s:"Discarding, learning, forwarding",
  x:"RSTP (802.1w) reduces the states to three. Discarding: no user frames are forwarded and no MACs are learned. Learning: still no forwarding but the MAC table is populated. Forwarding: full operation. Roles (root, designated, alternate, backup) are separate from states, so an alternate port is in discarding state. 'show spanning-tree' still prints BLK for a discarding alternate port, which confuses many candidates.",
  w:"Three states in RSTP, five in 802.1D."},
 {obj:"2.5.b",c:"stp",q:"Legacy 802.1D port states",a:"#Disabled · Blocking · Listening · Learning · Forwarding\nListening 15 s + learning 15 s = 30 s to forward",s:"Disabled, blocking, listening, learning, forwarding",
  x:"Classic STP uses five states. Blocking ports only receive BPDUs. A port moving to forwarding passes through listening (BPDUs processed, no MACs learned) and learning (MACs learned, no forwarding), each lasting one forward delay of 15 seconds. Recovering from an indirect failure also waits out the 20-second max age, so convergence could take 50 seconds. RSTP's handshake (proposal/agreement) is what removes those timers.",
  w:"Listening and learning: 15 s each by default."},
 {obj:"2.5.b",c:"stp",q:"RSTP link types",a:"#Point-to-point (full duplex) · Shared (half duplex) · Edge (PortFast)\nRapid transition works only on P2P and edge",s:"P2P, shared or edge — set by duplex and PortFast",
  x:"RSTP classifies each port's link. Full-duplex ports are point-to-point and can use the proposal/agreement handshake to forward almost instantly. Half-duplex ports are shared, as on a hub, and fall back to slower timer-based behaviour. Ports with PortFast are edge ports and forward at once. 'show spanning-tree' shows P2p, Shr or P2p Edge in the Type column; 'spanning-tree link-type point-to-point' can override the guess.",
  w:"Half duplex = shared = slow convergence."},
 {obj:"2.5",c:"stp",q:"Rapid PVST+ in one line",a:"#Cisco's per-VLAN version of RSTP (802.1w)\nspanning-tree mode rapid-pvst; one tree and root per VLAN",s:"Per-VLAN RSTP: a separate tree for each VLAN",
  x:"Rapid PVST+ runs a separate RSTP instance for every VLAN, each with its own root bridge, port roles and BPDUs. That lets you make SW1 root for odd VLANs and SW2 root for even VLANs so both uplinks carry traffic. It is enabled with 'spanning-tree mode rapid-pvst'; the older default on many IOS releases is PVST+ (per-VLAN 802.1D). The cost is CPU and BPDUs that scale with the number of VLANs.",
  w:"Per-VLAN root = per-VLAN load sharing."},
 {obj:"2.5",c:"stp",q:"STP timers: hello, max age, forward delay",a:"#Hello 2 s · Max age 20 s · Forward delay 15 s\nRoot bridge's values are used by the whole tree",s:"Hello 2, max age 20, forward delay 15",
  x:"The root sends BPDUs every 2 seconds (hello). In 802.1D a switch waits max age (20 s) without BPDUs before ageing out stored root information, and each listening and learning stage lasts forward delay (15 s). RSTP keeps the same configured values but every switch sends its own BPDUs and treats three missed hellos (6 s) as a lost neighbour. The root's timers are what all switches use, so tune them only on the root.",
  w:"2 / 20 / 15 — and RSTP gives up after 3 missed hellos."},
 {obj:"2.5.c",c:"stp",q:"#spanning-tree portfast",a:"#Access port goes straight to forwarding\nMakes it an RSTP edge port; for end hosts only",s:"Skips listening/learning on host-facing ports",
  x:"PortFast lets a port connected to a PC, printer or server move directly to forwarding when it comes up, so DHCP and logins do not time out during 30 seconds of listening and learning. In RSTP it marks the port as edge, and toggling an edge port does not trigger a topology change. The port still sends BPDUs, and if it receives one it loses edge status. Use 'spanning-tree portfast default' globally to apply it to all access ports.",
  w:"PortFast = host ports only, never switch-to-switch."},
 {obj:"2.5.c",c:"stp",q:"PortFast on a trunk port",a:"#spanning-tree portfast trunk\nPlain portfast is ignored on trunks; for servers or hypervisors",s:"Needs the trunk keyword: spanning-tree portfast trunk",
  x:"Plain 'spanning-tree portfast' only takes effect on access ports; on a trunk IOS ignores it. When a trunk goes to a host that cannot create a loop, such as a virtualisation server or a router-on-a-stick, 'spanning-tree portfast trunk' (or 'spanning-tree portfast edge trunk' in newer releases) makes it an edge port. IOS prints a warning to remind you that PortFast toward a switch or hub can cause temporary loops.",
  w:"Trunk to a server? Add the trunk keyword."},
 {obj:"2.5.d",c:"stp",q:"BPDU guard — what triggers it and what happens",a:"#Any BPDU received → port err-disabled\nspanning-tree bpduguard enable; usually paired with PortFast",s:"Err-disables the port when any BPDU arrives",
  x:"BPDU guard protects edge ports. If a port with BPDU guard receives any BPDU, meaning someone plugged in a switch, the port is shut down into err-disabled state and a log message is generated. Enable per port with 'spanning-tree bpduguard enable' or on all PortFast ports with 'spanning-tree portfast bpduguard default'. Recovery is manual (shutdown then no shutdown) unless 'errdisable recovery cause bpduguard' is set.",
  w:"BPDU on a guarded port = err-disabled until someone fixes it."},
 {obj:"2.5.d",c:"stp",q:"BPDU filter — global vs interface",a:"#Global: PortFast ports stop sending BPDUs, revert if one arrives\nInterface: sends and ignores none — STP effectively off",s:"Suppresses BPDUs; per-interface use disables STP there",
  x:"Enabled globally with 'spanning-tree portfast bpdufilter default', BPDU filter stops PortFast ports from sending BPDUs after a few at link-up; if a BPDU is received, the port loses PortFast and filtering and runs normal STP. Configured on an interface with 'spanning-tree bpdufilter enable', it neither sends nor processes BPDUs, which disables STP on that port and can allow a loop. BPDU guard and filter on one port: filter wins.",
  w:"Interface BPDU filter = STP off on that port. Handle with care."},
 {obj:"2.5.d",c:"stp",q:"Root guard",a:"#Superior BPDU on the port → root-inconsistent (blocked)\nRecovers by itself once superior BPDUs stop",s:"Blocks a port that hears a better root, auto-recovers",
  x:"Root guard is applied to designated ports facing switches that must never become root, such as access-layer downlinks or a customer edge. If a superior BPDU arrives claiming a better root, the port enters root-inconsistent state and stops forwarding, keeping the existing root in place. Unlike BPDU guard it does not err-disable: once the superior BPDUs stop, the port recovers on its own. Command: 'spanning-tree guard root'.",
  w:"Root guard = 'you may connect, you may not be root'."},
 {obj:"2.5.d",c:"stp",q:"Loop guard",a:"#BPDUs stop on a root or alternate port → loop-inconsistent\nPrevents a blocked port going forwarding after a unidirectional fault",s:"Blocks a non-designated port that stops getting BPDUs",
  x:"A root or alternate port normally keeps receiving BPDUs. If they stop because of a one-way link fault, plain STP would assume the path is gone and the port could become designated and forward, creating a loop. Loop guard instead moves the port to loop-inconsistent (blocking) until BPDUs return, then it recovers on its own. Enable with 'spanning-tree guard loop' or globally with 'spanning-tree loopguard default'. It is not used on edge ports.",
  w:"Root guard: too-good BPDUs. Loop guard: missing BPDUs."},
 {obj:"2.5.a",c:"stp",q:"Reading show spanning-tree: 'This bridge is the root'",a:"#Root ID equals Bridge ID; all ports Desg\nNo root port and a root cost of 0 on the root switch",s:"Root ID equals Bridge ID and every port is designated",
  x:"In 'show spanning-tree vlan 10', the Root ID block describes the elected root and the Bridge ID block describes the local switch. On the root they match and IOS prints 'This bridge is the root', with no Cost or Port line in the Root ID block, and every port is Desg FWD. On any other switch the Root ID block shows the total path cost and the local root port, which is the fastest way to trace the path to the root.",
  w:"Root ID block shows Port = your root port."},
 {obj:"2.5.b",c:"stp",q:"Port roles vs port states",a:"#Role = job in the topology · State = whether it forwards\nRoot/Desg/Altn/Back vs Discarding/Learning/Forwarding",s:"Role is the job; state is the forwarding status",
  x:"Roles are decided by the election: root port (best path to the root), designated port (best port onto a segment), alternate and backup (standby). States describe what the port does with frames right now: discarding, learning or forwarding in RSTP. A designated port is briefly in learning or discarding while it comes up, and an alternate port stays discarding. The exam likes to mix the two lists, so check which one the question asks for.",
  w:"'Alternate' is a role; 'discarding' is a state."},
 /* ---------- wlan (16) ---------- */
 {obj:"2.6",c:"wlan",q:"Autonomous AP architecture",a:"#Each AP is configured and managed on its own\nNo controller; trunks multiple VLANs for multiple SSIDs",s:"Self-contained APs managed one by one",
  x:"An autonomous AP holds its whole configuration and switches client traffic directly onto the wired network, so each SSID maps to a VLAN and the switch port is usually a trunk. It works well for a home or a single small office, but every AP is configured separately and features such as coordinated RF management and seamless roaming are limited. On the exam, 'standalone' or 'each AP configured individually' signals autonomous.",
  w:"Autonomous = no controller, one AP at a time."},
 {obj:"2.6",c:"wlan",q:"Lightweight AP and CAPWAP",a:"#AP tunnels to a WLC with CAPWAP\nUDP 5246 control (DTLS encrypted) · UDP 5247 data",s:"WLC-managed AP tunnelling over CAPWAP",
  x:"A lightweight AP downloads its configuration from a wireless LAN controller and keeps a CAPWAP tunnel to it. CAPWAP (an IETF standard) uses UDP 5246 for the control channel, which is always DTLS-encrypted, and UDP 5247 for the data channel, where client frames are encapsulated (encryption optional). Because traffic is tunnelled, the AP and WLC can sit in different subnets; the AP only needs an IP and a route to the controller.",
  w:"5246 control, 5247 data."},
 {obj:"2.6",c:"wlan",q:"Split-MAC — what the AP does vs the WLC",a:"#AP: real-time 802.11 work · WLC: management and policy\nAP: beacons, probes, ACKs, encryption · WLC: auth, roaming, RF",s:"AP does real-time radio tasks, WLC does management",
  x:"Lightweight designs split the 802.11 MAC functions. The AP handles time-critical tasks: transmitting beacons, answering probe requests, acknowledging frames, queuing and frame encryption. The WLC handles association and authentication, security policy, client roaming between APs, QoS policy and radio resource management (channel and power). Questions that ask which device handles RF management or authentication point to the WLC.",
  w:"Real-time = AP; decisions = WLC."},
 {obj:"2.6",c:"wlan",q:"Cloud-managed wireless",a:"#APs managed from a cloud dashboard (e.g. Meraki)\nData traffic stays local; only management goes to the cloud",s:"Dashboard in the cloud, client data switched locally",
  x:"In a cloud-managed architecture the controller function lives in a vendor-hosted dashboard. APs build an encrypted management tunnel out to it for configuration, monitoring and firmware, while client data is switched locally at the site. It suits organisations with many small branches and little on-site IT. If the internet link fails, existing configuration keeps working but changes and monitoring stop until it returns.",
  w:"Cloud manages; local wire carries the user data."},
 {obj:"2.6",c:"wlan",q:"AP mode: local",a:"#Default lightweight mode\nServes clients; all traffic tunnelled to the WLC over CAPWAP",s:"Default mode: serves clients, tunnels all to the WLC",
  x:"Local mode is the default for a lightweight AP. It serves clients on its channel while briefly scanning other channels for RRM and rogue detection, and it carries all client data inside CAPWAP to the WLC, which then switches it onto the right VLAN. Because everything is tunnelled, the AP's switch port can be a plain access port in the AP management VLAN. If the WLC becomes unreachable, a local-mode AP stops serving clients.",
  w:"Local mode = everything hairpins through the WLC."},
 {obj:"2.6",c:"wlan",q:"AP mode: FlexConnect",a:"#Can switch client traffic locally at a branch\nKeeps working if the WAN link to the WLC drops",s:"Local switching at a branch, survives WLC loss",
  x:"FlexConnect (formerly H-REAP) is for APs at remote sites where the WLC is across a WAN. Each WLAN can be centrally switched through CAPWAP or locally switched straight onto a branch VLAN, avoiding a trip across the WAN. If the controller becomes unreachable the AP moves to standalone and keeps serving locally switched WLANs. Local switching of several WLANs means the AP port is usually an 802.1Q trunk.",
  w:"Branch AP + WAN to the WLC = FlexConnect."},
 {obj:"2.6",c:"wlan",q:"AP modes monitor, sniffer, rogue detector",a:"#Monitor: scans, no clients · Sniffer: captures to a PC\nRogue detector: radios off, watches the wire for rogue MACs",s:"Scan only, packet capture, wired rogue detection",
  x:"None of these serve clients. Monitor mode scans all channels continuously for wireless IDS, rogue APs and location tracking. Sniffer mode captures 802.11 frames on one channel and forwards them to a remote PC running a protocol analyser such as Wireshark. Rogue detector mode turns the radios off and listens on the wired network, comparing ARP-learned MACs against the list of rogue clients reported by the controller.",
  w:"Sniffer sends captures to a PC; rogue detector watches the wire."},
 {obj:"2.6",c:"wlan",q:"AP modes SE-Connect and bridge/mesh",a:"#SE-Connect: dedicated RF spectrum analysis\nBridge/mesh: AP-to-AP wireless backhaul links",s:"Spectrum analysis vs AP-to-AP bridging",
  x:"SE-Connect (Spectrum Expert Connect) dedicates the radios to spectrum analysis, scanning all channels and sending raw RF data to remote analysis software, which reveals non-Wi-Fi interference such as microwaves or cameras. Bridge (mesh) mode lets APs build wireless links to each other: point-to-point or point-to-multipoint bridges between buildings, or a mesh where only root APs have a wired connection. Flex+Bridge combines mesh with FlexConnect.",
  w:"Interference hunting = SE-Connect; no cable to the AP = mesh."},
 {obj:"2.7",c:"wlan",q:"Switch port type for a local-mode AP vs FlexConnect AP",a:"#Local mode: access port · FlexConnect/autonomous: trunk\nLocal-mode client data stays inside CAPWAP",s:"Local mode on access; FlexConnect on a trunk",
  x:"A local-mode AP sends all client traffic to the WLC inside CAPWAP, so the switch sees only the AP's own management traffic in a single VLAN, and an access port is correct. An autonomous AP or a FlexConnect AP locally switching several SSIDs puts frames for different client VLANs onto the wire, so its port must be an 802.1Q trunk, with the AP management VLAN usually as native. Getting this backwards breaks either the AP join or client VLANs.",
  w:"Tunnelled traffic = access port; locally switched VLANs = trunk."},
 {obj:"2.7",c:"wlan",q:"WLC physical ports",a:"#Distribution system ports · service port · redundancy · console\nDistribution ports carry AP and client data (802.1Q trunks)",s:"Distribution, service, redundancy and console ports",
  x:"Distribution system ports are the WLC's data ports; they connect to the switch as 802.1Q trunks and carry CAPWAP to APs plus client traffic for every mapped VLAN. The service port is an out-of-band management port that supports no VLAN tagging and only management traffic. The redundancy port links two controllers for high availability (SSO), and the console port gives serial CLI access for initial setup and recovery.",
  w:"Data = distribution ports; out-of-band management = service port."},
 {obj:"2.7",c:"wlan",q:"WLC LAG — how it differs from a normal EtherChannel",a:"#All distribution ports in one bundle, mode on\nNo LACP or PAgP; switch side: channel-group X mode on",s:"One bundle of all distribution ports, static mode on",
  x:"Link aggregation on an AireOS WLC bundles all distribution system ports into one logical link; you cannot pick a subset or create several LAGs. The controller does not run LACP or PAgP, so the switch must use 'channel-group 1 mode on'. A protocol mode on the switch prevents the bundle from forming. LAG gives redundancy and bandwidth across ports, and enabling or disabling it requires a controller reboot.",
  w:"WLC LAG = static 'on', never active/passive or desirable/auto."},
 {obj:"2.7",c:"wlan",q:"PoE for access points",a:"#Switch powers the AP over the Ethernet cable\n802.3af 15.4 W · 802.3at (PoE+) 30 W · 802.3bt up to 90 W",s:"Power over the data cable from the switch",
  x:"Most APs are powered by Power over Ethernet from the access switch, so no outlet is needed at the ceiling. 802.3af provides up to 15.4 W at the port, 802.3at (PoE+) up to 30 W, and 802.3bt up to 60 or 90 W. Newer multi-radio APs may need PoE+ or more for all features; with too little power they boot with radios or ports disabled. 'show power inline' on the switch shows allocated and available power.",
  w:"AP up but radios limited? Check PoE budget and class."},
 {obj:"2.9",c:"wlan",q:"WLC GUI: creating a WLAN",a:"#WLANs > Create New: type, profile name, SSID, WLAN ID\nThen General tab: enable it and map an interface/VLAN",s:"Profile name, SSID and WLAN ID, then map an interface",
  x:"In the WLC GUI a new WLAN needs a profile name (a local label), the SSID that clients see, and a WLAN ID. On the General tab you set Status to Enabled and select the interface or interface group, which maps the SSID to a client VLAN through a dynamic interface on the controller. The broadcast SSID option and radio policy are also there. A WLAN that is created but not enabled will not be advertised.",
  w:"Profile name is internal; SSID is what users see."},
 {obj:"2.9",c:"wlan",q:"WLC GUI Security tab: Layer 2 options",a:"#WPA2 or WPA3 policy · AES encryption · auth key mgmt PSK or 802.1X\n802.1X needs RADIUS servers under the AAA Servers tab",s:"WPA2/WPA3 with PSK or 802.1X key management",
  x:"The Security tab's Layer 2 sub-tab sets the WPA policy (WPA2 or WPA3), the cipher (AES/CCMP) and authentication key management. PSK means one shared passphrase for everyone, typed into the PSK field. 802.1X (enterprise) authenticates each user against a RADIUS server, which you select on the AAA Servers sub-tab. WPA3-Personal uses SAE instead of PSK. Layer 3 options such as web authentication portals are on a separate sub-tab.",
  w:"PSK = shared passphrase; 802.1X = RADIUS per user."},
 {obj:"2.9",c:"wlan",q:"WLC QoS profiles",a:"#Platinum = voice · Gold = video · Silver = best effort · Bronze = background\nSilver is the default for a new WLAN",s:"Platinum=voice, gold=video, silver=BE, bronze=bulk",
  x:"Each WLAN is assigned a QoS profile on its QoS tab, which caps the priority its traffic can receive over the air and on the wire. Platinum is for voice, Gold for video, Silver for normal best-effort data and the default, and Bronze for background traffic such as guest networks. A voice SSID left on Silver will work but gets no priority over bulk data, a classic cause of choppy calls.",
  w:"Voice SSID belongs on Platinum."},
 {obj:"2.9",c:"wlan",q:"WLC GUI Advanced tab — common settings",a:"#Allow AAA Override · session timeout · client exclusion\nAlso max clients, DHCP required, coverage hole detection",s:"AAA Override, session timeout, client exclusion",
  x:"Allow AAA Override lets the RADIUS server return per-user attributes, such as a VLAN or QoS level, that override the WLAN's own settings. Session timeout sets how long a client stays associated before it must reauthenticate. Client exclusion blocks a client for a period after repeated failures such as wrong passwords. Other Advanced-tab options include the maximum clients per WLAN and requiring DHCP address assignment.",
  w:"RADIUS assigns the VLAN only if AAA Override is enabled."},
 /* ---------- mgmt (10) ---------- */
 {obj:"2.8",c:"mgmt",q:"Console access",a:"#Out-of-band serial access to the CLI\nDefault 9600 baud, 8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit",s:"Out-of-band serial CLI access, 9600 8-N-1",
  x:"The console port (RJ-45 rollover or USB) gives direct CLI access with no IP configuration at all, so it is how you configure a new device or recover one with a broken config or password. Terminal settings default to 9600 baud, 8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit, no flow control. Secure it under 'line console 0' with 'login local' or a password, and 'exec-timeout' to log idle sessions out.",
  w:"No IP yet? Console."},
 {obj:"2.8",c:"mgmt",q:"Telnet vs SSH",a:"#Telnet: TCP 23, cleartext · SSH: TCP 22, encrypted\nRestrict with transport input ssh on the VTY lines",s:"TCP 23 cleartext vs TCP 22 encrypted",
  x:"Both give remote CLI access through the VTY lines. Telnet sends everything, including the username and password, in cleartext over TCP 23, so anyone capturing traffic can read it. SSH encrypts the session over TCP 22 and authenticates the server with a key. Use SSH version 2 and allow only it with 'transport input ssh' under 'line vty 0 15' (or 0 4 on many routers). Telnet survives only on isolated labs.",
  w:"Captured password in a packet capture? Someone used Telnet."},
 {obj:"2.8",c:"mgmt",q:"Minimum steps to enable SSH on IOS",a:"#hostname · ip domain-name · crypto key generate rsa\nPlus a local user, login local and transport input ssh on VTY",s:"Hostname, domain name, RSA keys, user, VTY settings",
  x:"The RSA key is named from the hostname and domain name, so the default hostname 'Router' and a missing 'ip domain-name' block key generation. Generate keys with 'crypto key generate rsa' and choose at least 768 bits (2048 recommended) to support 'ip ssh version 2'. Then create 'username admin secret …', and under 'line vty 0 15' configure 'login local' and 'transport input ssh'. Verify with 'show ip ssh'.",
  w:"No domain name, no RSA key, no SSH."},
 {obj:"2.8",c:"mgmt",q:"HTTP vs HTTPS management",a:"#HTTP TCP 80 cleartext · HTTPS TCP 443 TLS-encrypted\nip http secure-server · no ip http server",s:"TCP 80 cleartext vs TCP 443 encrypted",
  x:"Many Cisco devices and every WLC offer a web GUI. Plain HTTP on TCP 80 sends credentials and configuration in cleartext; HTTPS on TCP 443 wraps the session in TLS. On IOS, enable the secure server with 'ip http secure-server' and disable the insecure one with 'no ip http server'. Like SSH versus Telnet, the exam answer for secure management is always the encrypted option.",
  w:"GUI on a router? ip http secure-server, no ip http server."},
 {obj:"2.8",c:"mgmt",q:"TACACS+ — key facts",a:"#TCP 49 · encrypts the whole payload · separates AAA\nCisco-developed; per-command authorization for admins",s:"TCP 49, full encryption, separate AAA functions",
  x:"TACACS+ was developed by Cisco and runs over TCP port 49. It encrypts the entire body of each packet, not just the password, and handles authentication, authorization and accounting as separate exchanges. That separation allows command-by-command authorization and detailed accounting of what each administrator typed, which is why TACACS+ is preferred for device administration access to routers and switches.",
  w:"Admins logging in to network gear = TACACS+."},
 {obj:"2.8",c:"mgmt",q:"RADIUS — key facts",a:"#UDP 1812 auth · 1813 accounting · encrypts only the password\nOpen standard; combines authentication and authorization",s:"UDP 1812/1813, password-only encryption, open standard",
  x:"RADIUS is an open IETF standard using UDP 1812 for authentication and 1813 for accounting (older systems use 1645/1646). Only the user's password is obscured in the Access-Request; other attributes travel in clear. Authentication and authorization are combined in a single Access-Accept, which can carry attributes such as a VLAN assignment. It is the standard choice for network access: 802.1X wired and wireless, VPN users.",
  w:"Users joining the network (802.1X) = RADIUS."},
 {obj:"2.8",c:"mgmt",q:"TACACS+ vs RADIUS — the quick contrast",a:"#TACACS+: TCP 49, full encryption, AAA separated\nRADIUS: UDP 1812/1813, password only, authN+authZ combined",s:"TCP and separate vs UDP and combined",
  x:"Exam questions on these two lean on four contrasts. Transport: TACACS+ uses TCP 49; RADIUS uses UDP. Encryption: TACACS+ encrypts the whole payload; RADIUS hides only the password. AAA: TACACS+ separates the three functions; RADIUS combines authentication and authorization. Origin: TACACS+ came from Cisco; RADIUS is an open standard. Use TACACS+ for device administration and RADIUS for end-user network access.",
  w:"T for TCP and Total encryption."},
 {obj:"2.8",c:"mgmt",q:"Point IOS logins at a TACACS+/RADIUS server with local fallback",a:"#aaa new-model · aaa authentication login default group tacacs+ local\nlocal is used only if the server does not respond",s:"aaa authentication login default group tacacs+ local",
  x:"'aaa new-model' turns on the AAA framework. A method list such as 'aaa authentication login default group tacacs+ local' tries the TACACS+ servers first and falls back to the local username database only if no server answers. A server that answers with a reject does not trigger fallback; the login simply fails. Always keep a local admin account so you can still log in when the AAA servers are unreachable.",
  w:"Fallback to local happens on no response, not on a wrong password."},
 {obj:"2.8",c:"mgmt",q:"VTY lines and management access",a:"#line vty 0 4 (0 15 on switches) — remote CLI sessions\nNeed login + password or login local, else no remote login",s:"Virtual terminal lines for Telnet/SSH sessions",
  x:"VTY lines are virtual terminals that accept Telnet and SSH sessions; each concurrent session uses one line. With no login method configured a VTY rejects connections with 'Password required, but none set'. Secure them with 'login local' and usernames, restrict protocols with 'transport input ssh', limit idle time with 'exec-timeout', and optionally restrict source addresses with 'access-class' and a standard ACL.",
  w:"access-class filters VTY; access-group filters interfaces."},
 {obj:"2.8",c:"mgmt",q:"Cloud-managed device management",a:"#Configure and monitor from a vendor web dashboard\nDevices call home over an encrypted tunnel; no per-device CLI",s:"Central web dashboard reached over the internet",
  x:"Cloud-managed devices, such as Meraki switches, APs and security appliances, are configured from a vendor-hosted web dashboard. Each device builds an outbound encrypted connection to the cloud, so no inbound ports or on-site controller are needed, and configuration templates apply to many sites at once. The trade-off is reliance on internet connectivity and the vendor's service for management changes; user traffic still flows locally.",
  w:"Zero-touch branches with no on-site IT = cloud-managed."},
];
